Hello,

1) I have a 533 MHz 164LX Alpha running RedHat Linux 5.1.

2) My modem is a Pro-Media 56000 (no, I have never heard of them either, but
it seems to have a Rockwell chip).

3) The modem works fine on an i386 system at work.

4) Using seyon I can converse with the modem without any trouble, and if I
manually enter the commands for my ISP connect sequence it logs on without
any problem.

5) When I run usernet the chat script seems to send exactly the required
commands to the modem, but when pppd starts it momentarily drops the DCD
line and the modem disconnects.  This only happens under 2.0.35, when I run
2.1.122 this particular problem does not occur.  Disabling DCD shows that
the connection is being correctly made.  Running pppd by hand seems to get
around this problem.  usernet also gave me grief on the i386 system, so this
particular issue may not be Alpha related.

6) When I establish a connection using seyon the modem responds with
"CONNECT 115200" if I have the baud rate set to 115200 and if I set it to a
lower speed then it responds with that speed.  However, when connecting
automatically the modem frequently responds with "CONNECT 9600" even though
pppd has definately been asked to use a higher baud rate.

7) When I finally establish a connection the link is VERY VERY slow.  I get
only about 20-30 bytes/s even from the ISPs own web and ftp servers.  DNS
lookups are slow and pinging the ISPs own machines gives some packet loss
and variable packet delays.  Under 2.1.122 the output of ifconfig gives:
          RX packets:100 errors:0 dropped:0 overruns:0
          TX packets:0 errors:4561 dropped:250 overruns:0
What is particularly wierd here is that Tx packets are clearly being sent,
since I can do DNS lookups and request web pages even if very slowly.  Under
2.0.35 the output of ifconfig seems correct.
I tried reducing the mtu and mru to 296 and this seemed to improve the
situation.  At one point I was getting 5kb/s out of the link, and then
suddenly, mid connection it slowed down again and gave me all the old
symptoms.

All suggestions greatly appreciated.

