In comp.os.linux.alpha you write:

I just got RedHat 5.1 installed.   My linux book says that I need to edit
the inittab file to make X windows come up automatically.  But it doesn't
say how to edit it.

Usually this is runlevel 5.  Have a look at /etc/inittab; one of the first
non-commented lines will be something like "id:2:initdefault: .  Change
the "2" to a "5".

Personally, I wouldn't do this, or recommend it to you until you've gotten
to know the OS a bit better, but it's up to you.

I looked for emacs...couldn't find it.  Isn't that what I need to edit the
file?

You can use any editor you like.  emacs, jed, joe, vi, ae, ee, jove,
nedit, vim, elvis, or about a dozen others...  The quick way to find one
particular one is (for example) "which emacs".  You'll get a prompt reply
if it's on your system.  If it's not, and you want it, just install it :-)

Also, the only way I got linux to recognize my monitor

[What??]

 was to give the
highest specs my monitor docs had.  The RH instructions said to go lower to
insure that I didn't smoke my monitor.  Now I am looking at a 1280x1024
screen.  I'd just as soon look at 800x600.  How do I make the adjustment and
not lose my screen?

This is actually a question about how to configure X.  I've always found
that the best way to do it is by running "xf86config".  You'll need to
know a few details about your monitor (horiz/vert refresh rates) and video
card.  You can then set the default to 800x600 (I use that, too) by
altering the "Modes" lines near the end of your config file
(/etc/X11/XF86Config) so that the first mode listed is "800x600".  (You
can probably just alter the file you've already got.)  You can also change
from one mode to another with Ctl-Alt-+ (or -) on the keypad, to see how
they look first.

Again, be clear.  If linux indeed wants to offer a product easy enough to
install and run as win95, then it needs a support group to do it.

Now look here, young man:  Linux Usenet support is excellent, but you have
to use it correctly.  You've posted this to the Linux-Alpha group, and not
one
of your questions has anything whatsoever to do with the Alpha.  You'll
get much better answers about setup in the "setup" group, and about X in
the X group.  And making potentially rude remarks may well get you a lot
of rude answers.  Please remember that you're asking people to volunteer
their time to help you.

P.S.  Have *you* ever actually tried to *install* Win95?  Installing Linux
is much easier.  But maybe I'm wrong; I've only been using it for four
years...






