I just got my AS 400 4/233 running RH 5.1 (a very similar system), but 
via the ARC console (and generous help from Jay Eserbrook).

To begin with, you probably should update your firmware.  This, I believe,
will give you the ARC console, though it is probably already present
(see below for how to switch consoles); the ARC console in current
versions of firmware makes it clear how to get back to the SRM console;
I'm not sure it was so kind in older versions.

See: ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/Alpha/firmware/index.html
for current firmware.

The bad news is that RH 5.1 is broken in various ways on the installation 
path (5.0 was alot better, apparently); the good news from Jay running 
5.2 field testis that 5.2 should be much better (directly booting off 
the CD).  I've asked him to prepare an errata for RedHat 5.1. You can 
poke him.

I'll try to wing it from memory...

You should get the Alpha addendum (I presume you have).

If you are at the SRM console on that machine, the way to get to the ARC 
console is to "set os_type NT" at the console ">>>" prompt, and power 
cycle. (You may very well have an ARC console there, and not know it is 
there already).  This is amazingly obscure.

The installation addendum does not make it clear that the first floppy 
you make is a FAT floppy (when using the ARC console), onto which you 
copy the appropriate MILO file (which you can get from Digital's Web site, 
or an older version off of the CD) and linload.exe.  Finding linload.exe 
on your Alpha CD is another minor task left to the confused reader.

Then you follow the instructions to tell the ARC console to boot
from the first floppy, and continue into the second and third
(which get generated via the dd or the PC utility on the CD whose
name escapes me).

If Digital UNIX was on a disk, and you plan to install RH over it, Linux 
can get confused by the partition information left over. Since I wasn't 
going to try to dual boot (having a different system to run Digital UNIX 
on), we blew the partition table away; if you don't, you can get into 
trouble (as I did the first time).  In theory, one can find a dd command and 
use it at an appropriate moment (just before entering FDISK, toggle over 
to vertual console 2 via "alt-F2"); in practice, we just used "echo " 
...(a very long string)... " > dev/sda", because we found it hard to find 
the dd command.


There is information in the addendum about dual booting Linux and Digital 
UNIX, but I haven't tried it. 

The other problem I had was having 2 TGA displays installed; the
console selects the opposite screen from Linux to put the boot messages
on; Jay now knows about this little gotcha (read bug).  I had to move
cables back and forth (since I've lost the key to my machine and
couldn't remove the second display; gotta break into the system sometime
soon...).

The installation then went without further trouble to speak of.
We then updated all the RPM's released since RH5.1 shipped.

We created a small partition, as suggested, for putting the MILO and
linload.exe on, again, with the ARC console, it is a FAT file system
partition.  CAREFULLY follow the setup in section 1.5.5.1 of the
ARC console, paying attention to the OSLOADOPTIONS boot selection.
Now my system boots up via the ARC console all the way to Linux.

			Hope this helps.
				- Jim Gettys




