And he did try ./program, too!

> Likely you are missing a shared library somehow. 
> The program is there, but since the shared libraries are
> part of the program, and can't be found, you get the file
> not found error. I forget now how I fixed that when I saw
> it.

You can run ldd on your executable to find out what shared libraries
it is trying to link with.

You can then set the $LD_LIBRARY_PATH environment variable to tell ld
where to look for libraries (ie export
LD_LIBRARY_PATH="/lib:/usr/lib:/usr/local/lib"), if this turns out to
be your problem.

