Well, I can answer your first couple of questions...

To format the DOS partition, I used "mkfs"...

o  Get the partition size (in blocks) from the fdisk "p" option to print the
partition table.
o  Enter "mkfs -tmsdos /dev/sda# <part size>" where # is the number of the
partition, and <part size> is the size of the DOS partition in blocks
o  Put the MILO floppy in the drive, mount both it and the newly formatted
partition, and copy the files from the floppy to the DOS partition.

Putting together a working ARC boot entry is a tad more challenging.  Here's
mine:

LOADIDENTIFIER          Redhat Linux Alpha 5.1
SYSTEMPARTITION      scsi(0)disk(0)rdisk(0)partition(3)
OSLOADER                    scsi(0)disk(0)rdisk(0)partition(3)\linload.exe
OSLOADPARTITION      scsi(0)disk(0)rdisk(0)partition(3)
OSLOADFILENAME      \avanti.arc
OSLOADOPTIONS         boot sda1:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda1

As you can see from the above, my DOS partition is "3".  It took some trial
and error to figure this out, since fdisk said it was partition 6 or
something like that, which I knew couldn't be right (partition table format
only supports 4 primary partitions).  The MILO image is named "avanti.arc"
on my AS200 and "udb.arc" on my Multia.  It really doesn't matter, so long
as you give ARC the proper filename and have the proper image for your
system configuration.  The OSLOADOPTIONS are passed to the MILO image on
boot and provide it with the boot string so you don't have to type it in.

As for rebuilding the kernel, I've had some problems with that.  I'm still
downloading updates from Redhat's ftp server to get everything patched up.
If I figure something out, I'll let you know.

Hope all this helps. :)

