I'm trying to configure my Linux/Alpha box (RedHat 4.0, Linux 2.0.32)
to run as a gateway between an ethernet LAN and an ADSL connection to
an ISP.  I think that in principle this should be easy (although I'm
far from being a network admin), but I'm having problems getting the 2
network adapters to be recognized simultaneously.  I have an SMC ISA
EzCard10 and an ancient NE2000 knockoff (also ISA -- I've been using
it in a small LAN for years with no problem, so I don't think it's a
NE2000 compat problem).  Both seem to run fine under the good old
NE2000 driver, but only one at a time -- on boot, the probe finds one
or the other but not both.  I've tried the incantation in the
Multiple-Ethernet mini-HOWTO:

  boot sda4:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda4 ether=0,0,eth1

and the variant (the SMC docs claim that IO addrs from 0x200-0x3E0 are
acceptable, and IRQ 10 seems to be available according to
/proc/interrupts):

  boot sda4:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda4 ether=10,0x200,eth1

but no dice.  Neither card is jumpered and I'm a bit lost on how to
force them into particular IRQ/IOaddr configs.  The SMC card claims to
be PnP, but the other far predates that technology (and I don't know
how to control PnP under Linux anyway).

Anyway, any clues would be deeply appreciated.  Please, please, please
respond via email -- I try to keep up with the news, but get swamped
easily...

Many thanks in advance for any/all assistance.

P.S. On a separate, but related, note -- what is the appropriate
assignment of IP addrs to the two network adapters on the gateway box?
(Told you I'm not a network admin... ;-) We have static IP addrs for
the machines on the internal LAN, and obviously the adapter connected
to the ADSL line has to have the number for the gateway machine, but
can the same number also be assigned to the other network adapter in
the same box?  Or can I get away with assigning that adapter a private
number from one of the reserved local network address blocks
(e.g. 192.168.0.1), and pointing the other machines on the LAN to that
as gateway?

