Peter Mott <mott@mathworks.com> wrote:
>I'm having trouble with dselect/dpkg dependencies and this CD. There are 
>many packages that depend on libc6, but that package isn't available on 
>the CD. Instead, a newer revision, libc6.? is on the is available. 

That's libc6.1.

>So let's assume the newer version is backward compatible. Can I get all 
>of the packages that depend on libc6 to accept this newer libc and how do 
>I do this? Or, perhaps someone can point me to where I can get the alpha 
>binary package for libc6?

Actually, there was never a libc6 for Debian/Alpha. The problem was/is
that some package maintainers had hardcoded the dependencies on the
shared libraries, instead of letting the packaging system figure those
out automatically. So, in all of these cases, you can safely override
the libc6 dependency; all packages will work with the libc6.1.

The package ldso is somewhat special. It's a dummy package that is not
used by anything, but it's currently marked as "required" due to i386
idiosyncracies.

Overriding the dependencies may be somewhat difficult with dselect;
instead, you may want to install those "problematic" packages by hand,
with "dpkg -i --override-dependencies ..../bla_1.2-3.deb".  You can
install more than one package with one dpkg call, just supply the
multiple filenames.

>This is my first debian installation ever and I'm hoping that I can 
>create a 'local' access method, in addition to the CD, to replace the 
>libc6 dependencies or make libc6 available.

The current state of Debian/Alpha is rapidly improving, I believe there
is at this moment only one or two packages left with the incorrect
dependency, and those should be resolved in about a week. Also,
dependencies on old libraries (those in the oldlibs section) are also
being phased out. By the time Debian 2.1 is released (middle November?)
things should be a lot more comfortable.

For the time being, you could point dselect at a debian mirror, and let
it upgrade your system from there (if you have enough bandwidth...)


