Anton Ertl wrote:

> In article <6u7ac0$n7l$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>,
>  pdavids@sig.net writes:
> > o  Do any of the alpha kernels support UltraDMA (e.g. a Promise add-in UDMA
> > controller card)?
>
> Interesting thought. Would be more cost-effective than going SCSI in
> many settings.

Actually, kernels 2.1.115 and newer support the CMD646 IDE DMA chipset for
164LX.  To use it:

1. Go in and edit drivers/block/Config.in and just under the line about NS87415
support add the line:

  bool '     CMD646 chipset support (EXPERIMENTAL)' CONFIG_BLK_DEV_CMD646

2. Re-configure the kernel, making sure to:
 - Answer 'y' to "Prompt for development and/or incomplete code/drivers", which
should be the first question.
 - Answer 'y' to "Enhanced IDE/MFM/RLL disk/cdrom/tape/floppy support".
 - Answer 'y' to "Generic PCI IDE chipset support", "Generic PCI bus-master DMA
support", and "Use DMA by default when available".
 - Finally, answer 'y' to "CMD646 chipset support".

3. make dep; make clean; make boot; make modules; make modules_install (though
probably not all on one line)

4. Reboot, and sit back!

(Parts of that procedure came from Dave Gilbert.)

On a dd time test, I get less than 1/3 the wall clock time, and about 1/15 the
CPU time of my old non-DMA kernel.

Before:
time dd < linux-2.1.108.tar.gz > qqq.killme
22583+1 records in
22583+1 records out
0.063u 7.335s 0:12.85 57.5%     0+0k 0+0io 1488pf+0w

After:
time dd < linux-2.1.108.tar.gz > qqq.killme
22583+1 records in
22583+1 records out
0.044u 0.388s 0:03.87 10.8%     0+0k 0+0io 1488pf+0w

As they say in Jamaica, "Zeeen!"

