it's all a matter of volume.  since most of the new mass-market pc's take
sdram (which, please note, isn't really "6 times faster" in an absolute sense)
that's what's cheapest nowadays.  this sort of thing happened with edo vs.
fast page mode ram last year (the former used to command a premium price but
eventually sunk somewhat below the cost of "regular" fpm) and, on the simm
level, with 72-pin vs. 30-pin simms (now *that's* ancient) before that.
sometimes this has really silly effects; for instance, i've seen places 
advertising 70ns (72-pin) 4x32 simms for 50% more than the equivalent 60ns 
memory! of course 60ns memory can be used wherever 70ns can, but not everyone
reading the manuals for their (older) computer realized that.  and 30-pin
simms go for ridiculously high per-meg prices nowadays.

the memory needed for the multia is *x36 (rather than *x32) standard
jedec simms, where *=2 for 8M, *=4 for 16M, 8 for 32M, etc. and fortunately 
they aren't really *that* ancient or hard to find, or overpriced if you
know where to look.  fpm memory is going for about $1/meg or a little less
nowadays (edo seems to be bottoming at about $0.80/meg).  you're going to
be wanting a second 8x36 (32M) simm, i suppose.  now, a run-of-the-mill
8x32 simm has sixteen 16-megabit (4Mx4) chips on it.  because of the way
the simms are organized, the 8x36's (at least the ones i have in front of me
right now) actually have twenty-four such chips (not just eighteen.) given
that, one would hope the price for an 8x36 would be at most 1.5 times that
for an 8x32.  i paid $45/ea the other day at a local (socal) fry's (gaaaack)
which seems reasonable (equivalent to 94 cents/meg).  i know for sure that
i could gotten the memory for less if i hadn't been in a hurry by (a) waiting
till my next trip to the bay area (but hell, gas costs money too); (b) looking
up the lowest price on pricewatch.com, which at this very moment quotes $37
from "Dynasty Express," a price that they do indeed seem to be offering 
on http://www.dynastyexpress.com/advscripts/programs/partlist.asp?CAT=MEM,
at least if you want to accept a 30-day warranty and risk that the memory
will "die nasty" later.  six bucks more for the lifetime warranty.  but hell,
shipping costs money too.

long and the short of it:  you should definitely be able to get a better price
than sixty bucks for that 8x36.  try pricewatch.com if you don't have a 
local retailer.  i don't think prices are all that unreasomable -- they seem
in line with the current approx $0.80-$1/meg cost of generic fpm in any format
given the "extra" chips.

btw i also ordered one of the "onsale" multias (multiae?) and haven't gotten 
it yet, about which i'm exceedingly pissed -- cpu micromart, which is supposed
to "fulfill" the order, is completely and totally unresponsive to my phone
calls and email.   onsale has been very good about it but they can't do
anything until they themselves make contact with cpu micromart.  i'm highly
torqued at the fact that if you go to cpu micromart's "order status" page
and enter your order number, it says "your order has been received in our
system but tracking information is not yet available."  i figured out that you
can put ANYTHING into the query blank and get the same answer.  placebo
anyone?  grrrrr..

