In a previous article, Tony Hernandez <dbsaint@ufl.edu> said:
>drive, memory and cdrom... i was wondering what my options were as far
>as having a CDROM and a Hard drive.
>and memory... im not sure if you an have a SCSI cdrom AND a SCSI hard
>drive also..is there any ways to do this? i only have one scsi connector
>, and im under the impression that its a 50 pin..

The beauty of SCSI is that it's a chain.  Each external SCSI device has two
SCSI connectors on it, one to connect to the device before it on the chain,
and one to connect to the one after it.  The bad part is that the last device
on the chain has to be terminated.  Some devices have a dip switch or jumper
to make it terminate, but otherwise you have to buy an ACTIVE (not passive)
terminator.  I've seen them for $11 on the web.  The cables that go from
drive to drive are also available for cheap on the web if you search for
"daisy chain" and "centronics".  You're looking for short cables (I like 1.5'
long ones) that have 50 pin "centronics" style male connectors on each end.
The first cable you need though, from the unit to the first external SCSI, has
a 50 pin "compact" or "SCSI-2" connector on one end, and a 50 pin "centronics"
style male connector on the other.


I have 2 hard disks, a SCSI CD-ROM, and a SCSI Zip drive all hooked up to my
Multia/UDB's one SCSI port.

>i also believe i have one PCI slot, are there any scsi cards that can
>gimmie another SCSI connector ?

You don't need it.  Save the PCI slot for a better graphics card or a 100
megabit ethernet card.

>Is this a good idea? has anyone ever done it? What kind of memory does
>this take, i treid some spare EDO i had laying around, but i got the 14

You *must* use "True Parity" RAM.  It's a bit more expensive than EDO, but
it's still not all that expensive.  Look on www.pricewatch.com for "True
Parity".

