I'm not an expert on Linux's SCSI subsystem, but I have tons of experience
with SCSI under other Un*xes and this sounds like a cabling/termination
problem to me.	I noticed that you said that NT seems to work.	My experience
is that under Un*x, the SCSI bus timing seems to be much tighter than under
NT, making it more prone to lockups.  I guess we've spent a little too much
time tweaking those drivers for performance, while Micro$oft just says to buy
a faster CPU. In any case, I would recommend that you do the following:

o  Make sure your external SCSI cable is of high quality.  Granite Digital
(www.scsipro.com) makes some of, if not _the_, best ones around. o  Make sure
your external cable is no more than 1 meter long.  Long SCSI cables can cause
tons of problems. o  Make sure you're using an active terminator on the
external bus.  Again, Granite Digital sells these and they're awesome. o 
Make sure the bus isn't over-terminated.  My AS200 has an active terminator
on the end of it's internal SCSI cable.  If the Diff->SE interface converter
terminates the cable, too, then you need to remove the terminator plug from
the end of the internal cable. o  If all else fails, try moving the CD-ROM
drive from it's external case to the internal cable temporarily.  If it still
doesn't work, it may be the CD-ROM drive or the interface converter is a tad
flaky.

Hope these suggestions help...


