Posted again (corrected this time thanks to Brian King)

HOWTO for installing RH5.1 on an Onsale AS200 4/233

> Steps to follow:
> 1. Do not trust the drivers given by RedHat
> 2. You need 4 disks (not 3 as specified by the installation manual)
> 3. Get the milo for your correct machine from
> ftp://gatekeeper.dec.com/pub/Digital/Linux-Alpha/Miniloader/latest-Images
> by getting milo.dd from the directory that corresponds to your machine.
> Once you download milo.dd you need to rawrite it to a formatted floppy
> if you are in a DOS mode or dd it if you are in unix (dd if=milo.dd
> of=/dev/fd0)
> 4. Get a kernel that corresponds to your machine from
> ftp://ftp.redhat.com/pub/redhat/redhat-5.1/alpha/kernels/ Be careful
> that this is a zipped image. This zipped image needs to be COPIED as is
> to a formatted disk. Litterally copied! you can rename the file you
> copied to floppy to vmlinux.gz so that you do not get confused.
> 5. from the directory
> ftp://ftp.redhat.com/pub/redhat/redhat-5.1/alpha/images/ you need the
> file ramdisk.img. This file you need to rawrite/dd it to a formatted
> disk.
> 6. This is the missing link for you. From that same directory download
> the file that corresponds the the same name as the kernel you
> downloaded. i.e. if you downloaded noritake-s.gz you need to get from
> this directory noritake-s.img and rawrite/dd it to the 4th floppy
> 7. I suppose that by now you have moved all the files to disks the way i
> explained. Remember to rename the .gz file that you downloaded to
> vmlinux.gz
> 8. power-cycle your alpha. Insert the first floppy in the drive and type
> boot dva0 at the SRM prompt.
> 9. at the Milo prompt type exactly the following command:
> boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1 prompt_ramdisk=1
> I suppose that you have remembered to RENAME the zipped image to
> vmlinux.gz when you COPIED it to the floppy
> 10. When prompted for a root disk insert the disk that has the
> ramdisk.img
> 11. At this point you should enter the installation and the RH5.1 manual
> will be very exact. Uptil a point where it will ask you to insert root
> disk in the drive. DO NOT INSERT THE DISK THAT CONTAINS THE ZIPPED
> IMAGE. instead insert the 4th disk.

Joseph Saab
Systems Administrator
Epoch Internet


