I recently purchased 3 of the Onsale AS200 4/233's (16Mb, PowerStorm
 3D10 card, etc.). Since I have some local machines running DEC Unix
 and experience with the SRM console, I decided to try to install/boot
 from SRM. I have/had absolutely no previous experience with Linux.
 Contrary to some of the postings I've seen here and elsewhere, much
 of this was actually pretty easy. However, I do have an important
 question, please see the bottom!!

 As per postings about installing from the ARC console, I first
 updated the firmware as below. I don't know if this is really
 necessary for an SRM install, but it doesn't seem to hurt.

 UPDATE SRM FIRMWARE:

 1. download "mkboot.exe" and "as200_v6_8.exe" from
       ftp://gatekeeper.dec.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware
 2. rename "as200_v6.8.exe" to "fwupdate.exe"
 3. (on a Win95 machine) execute the following command while
       a 3 1/2" Win95 formatted floppy is in drive A:
       c:\mkboot c:\fwupdate.exe (substitute correct path to
                                  both mkboot and fwupdate.exe)
 4. follow instructions
 5. place the disk into the AlphaStation and execute "b -fl 0,a0 dva0"
 6. on the menu that will eventually come up, select the SRM update
 7. after the process has completed, turn off power, wait 20
seconds,           then restart

 Since we have the RedHat Linux/Alpha CD's, an external CD to connect
 to the Onsale box (actually a DEC RRD42, but I'm sure many others
 work...), and a nearby machine with floppy and CD running DEC Unix,
 we used the procedure below.

 INSTALL REDHAT 5.1 FROM LINUX/ALPHA CD:

 1. on another Unix machine, in our case running DEC Unix, prepare
        two floppies using the dd command; one each containing the
        avanti-s.img and ramdisk.img files from the RedHat 5.1 CD.
        our exact command to write the floppies was:
              dd if=avanti-s.img of=/dev/fd0 bs=1440k, and
        similar for the ramdisk.img floppy. See appendix B of the
        RedHat 5.1 Installation manual.
 2. put the kernel floppy (avanti-s.img) in the AS200 and at the
        SRM prompt issue, all on one line:
              boot dva0 -file vmlinux.gz -flags "root=/dev/fd0
                  load_ramdisk=1 prompt_ramdisk=1"
 3. this will boot the kernel and eventually ask you to insert the
        other floppy (ramdisk.img)
 4. now you get the Linux installation menu (I said it was easy...)
        and I did a very standard install, including all the RPM
        packages and partitioning my 2Gb disk into only 2 partitions;
        a 1.7 Gb / (root) partition on partition 1, and a 300 Mb swap
        area on 5.
 5. continue with the complete installation, using the defaults that
        the setup detects for the video card, etc. I've had no
        problems whatsoever with the video to this point (but then
        I don't yet have X running, of course....)
 6. finally, when asked to put the kernel disk back in, put in the
        avanti-s.img (kernel) floppy and hit ENTER
 7. this completes the installation, and gives the machine back to
        the SRM console

 Now for my question.... I'm quite certain that I now have Linux
 correctly installed on my disk, but I can't figure out how to boot
 from the SRM prompt! I've tried various permutations and mutations
 of the command (vmlinux replaced by cmlinux, 1 replaced by 2, etc.):

                boot dka0 -file 1/vmlinux.gz

 No version of the above seems to find a correct bootstrap. Does
 anyone know where the Linux kernel resides on my disk at this point?
 And can I boot it directly from SRM? From what I can figure out in
 retrospect, it seems that the people who install from ARC console
 are making some tiny "DOS" partition where they put a boot/load
 program that is first invoked and which then boots Linux from their
 root partition. Is this correct, and if so, do I actually need to
 reinstall and put some SRM equivalent of this in some tiny, artificial
 partition on my disk as well?

                Any help much appreciated, Jay P.

