I posted this under another thread where it may not get noticed, so
I'll repeat the key part of the posting here....

Like many others, I recently got one of the Onsale AS200 boxes with
PowerStorm 3D10 graphics, etc. After some playing around, and using
some suggestions gleened from this group, we finally were able to
load full RH5.1 with all packages including X via the SRM console
(using avanti-s.img from the RH5.1 CD, aboot, etc.). We have now
had the machine up for about a day and have had NO problems at all
with the PowerStorm graphics, or anything else, locking up the
machine as reported here. We've tried a number of things, including
running the "Japanese" mozilla executable often mentioned in this
group, and everything works fine! The only other thing we did,
besides installing an old 2Gb hard disk, was increase the memory
from the the Onsale 16 Mb to 16+64=80Mb and hook up an external CD.

An outline of our full procedure for installing and booting under
SRM looks like: (1) upgrade the SRM firmware using a floppy built
using mkboot.exe and as200_v6_8.exe from the DEC site, (2) boot
Linux using floppies prepared via dd'ing avanti-s.img and
ramdisk.img from the RH5.1 CD /images directory, (3) install RH5.1
from the CD in the standard way; you must partition the disk with
fdisk instead of Disk Druid, and use it to start the first partition
about 1 Mb into the disk, i.e., the first 1 Mb or so is not in any
partition, (4) after installation you get back the SRM console,
(5) boot from the avanti-s.img kernel floppy, and once you get to
the hard disk use fdisk under the -b option to write a BSD disk
label with the partitions exactly identical to the ones you set
up during the CD installation, (6) use the aboot package installed
on the hard disk during CD installation to write the aboot bootloader
to the empty 1 Mb area at the start of the disk; this is described
in the Alpha addendum booklet that comes with RH5.1, (7) shut the
machine down to SRM console and set the BOOT_DEV, BOOT_OSFLAGS, etc.
flags at console level to point to the hard disk, root partition,
and so forth, (8) from now on just type "boot" at the SRM prompt
to boot directly into Linux from the hard disk, and enjoy!

This is probably neither harder nor easier than the corresponding
and vaguely similar procedures needed to get the box up under ARC
console as previously reported in this group. But it does work....
If anyone has trouble send me email with -details- of your problem
and I'll try to help, unless I start getting swamped with queries.

                      Best wishes, Jay Ponder

