Hi, I recently installed Debian/Alpha on a 533LX using a Pentium to
bootstrap-install the IDE hard-disk.

I downloaded from ftp.debian.org the slink distribution

I downloaded linload.exe, milo, lx164.gz images from 

ftp.redhat.com/pub/redhat/redhat-5.1/alpha/

Partitioned an IDE disk in the Pentium with 6MB Fat as 1st primary and
rest of space as ext2

I mounted the disk with fat partition (/dev/hdb1) under /floppy, ext2
partition (/dev/hdb2) under /mnt

I copied linload.exe and milo to fat partition , lx164.gz to root
dir of
ext2 partition

Still on the Pentium (debian Linux) I used dpkg to unpack the 'base' files
into the disk I intended to install in the Alpha (/dev/hdb).  I copied
files from Pentium /dev to Alpha /dev by: cp -a /dev/* /mnt/dev

Now is where the fun started; I had to do a manual install of the packages
that were critical to getting to a login prompt (using the Pentium).  I
don't remember the exact packages but sysvinit was an important one.
libc6.1 was also critical.  As long as there were boot files like inittab
and /sbin/init and appropriate /dev/tty's and /dev/hda's, and some login
files then I could get a login prompt.

Now I went through the process of swapping the disk (and video card,
Diamond Stealth 64) between the Pentium and Alpha several times
making some changes that unfortunately I can't exactly remember what but
enjoyed the challenge, until I eventually won a login prompt.

Another important thing is that milo booted linux with a line that
included root=/dev/hda2,  ie

MILO> boot hda2:lx164.gz root=/dev/hda2

There were still lots of error messages and 'unexpected interrupt xx'
messages but the login prompt still appeared

My next hurdle now is to get the aha152x module loaded so that I can use
my internal scsi zip drive.

Not too specific to spoil fun but I hope this helped

Nick.


