Ok,
Get 4 dos formatted disks.
Go to the redhat site and get a file called rawrite.exe that you will
need to run on a dos machine.
Also you need the following files:
1-ftp://gatekeeper.dec.com/pub/Digital/Linux-Alpha/Miniloader/latest-Images/Noname/milo.dd
2-ftp://gatekeeper.dec.com/pub/Digital/Linux-Alpha/Kernels/Test-pre34-11b/NONAME.gz
3-ftp://ftp.redhat.com/pub/redhat/redhat-5.1/alpha/images/ramdisk.img
4-ftp://ftp.redhat.com/pub/redhat/redhat-5.1/alpha/images/noname.img

Now Label the first disk: MILO
Now Label the second disk: BOOT
Now Label the third disk: ROOT
Now Label the third disk: FIRST DISK <---- very important

Use rawrite.exe to put milo.dd on MILO
Use a simple copy command to COPY NONAME.gz BOOT
Use rawrite to put ramdisk.img on ROOT
Use rawrite to put noname.img on FIRST DISK

Now power-up your Multia and insert the MILO floppy. At the prompt type
the following and press enter:
boot dva0

It will boot ad then give you a MILO> prompt. Insert the BOOT disk and
type the following and press enter:
boot fd0:NONAME.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1 prompt_ramdisk=1

It will boot the kernel and asks for the ramdisk or the ROOT disk.
Insert that disk in and press enter.

Now you should be in the RedHat installation menu. If you have a network
card in and you are connected to the network and know your IP and
gateway and nameserver, you can choose the FTP installation.
Alternatively you can choose the CDROM installation. At some point after
the transfer of files is done, you will be prompted to insert the BOOT
disk or the FIRST disk. Insert that disk which i told you to label FIRST
DISK and then press enter. You should be all set.

Good luck.

Joseph Saab
Systems Administrator
Epoch Internet

