rsimpson@ewrcsdra.Cut_This_Out.demon.co.uk wrote:

> 1) Get the ffmath library -
> http://people.frankfurt.netsurf.de/Joachim.Wesner/.  This made a BIG
> difference to our Fortran codes.

Indeed !  Thanks to Joachim for this effort !

> 2) I understand that haifa does not work under Alpha with egcs 1.0.3.  I
> deduce this from the fact that one of the changes under egcs 1.1a is listed
> as "haifa now works under Alpha".

Well, in the egcs 1.0.x series, the HAIFA instruction scheduler was included 
as an untested option.  Unfortunately, (AFAIUnderstand), Red Hat build their 
egcs 1.0.2-8 with HAIFA enabled, which introduced subtle bugs in the compiler 
- yeah, you just can't win.  Goes to show that "works for us" isn't a 
guarantee with compilers ...

> 3) Switching to 1.1a did not improve the speed of my code until I enabled
> the -fno-emulate-complex option whereupon I got a big improvement.  My
> results files were identicle with and without this option.

I start to get an idea when -fno-emulate-complex works and when it doesn't.

<WILD_SPECULATION>

One of the main problems with the internal representation of Single Precision 
Complex Data in the "middle" end of the compiler - i.e. the routines between 
the various front-ends and backends is that an item that's 64-bits wide is 
kept as a single item.  On targets that are basically 64-bit architectures 
(like the Alpha, some Sparc and some MIPS targets), this interferes with the 
way two 32-bit parts are handled vis-a-vis the 64-bit registers.  One way to 
see this is to try to compile the LAPACK distribution from ftp.netlib.org 
with -fno-emulate-complex on an Alpha - it dies in one of the first single 
precision complex routines with an abort in gen_low_part; i.e. it tries to 
insert or extract a (32-bit) part of a 64-bit register that doesn't support 
32-bit parts (like the floating point registers on an Alpha).

</WILD_SPECULATION>

So, it *might* be that for DOUBLE COMPLEX there's actually no problem.

There's hope that this will be solved in the not to distant future, because 
glibc (as a consequence of having to move to the C9X Standard) is going to 
use the internal complex representation more often.

HTH,

