peltz@jaka.ece.uiuc.edu (Steve Peltz) writes:

> I DON'T think it is hardware problems, but I'm not sure of any way to
> reliably test memory.

Memory problems will often cause a kernel-recompile to segfault. It
would be nice to have a better test.

> It LOOKS like it might be some problem in the
> swapping mechanism (note: there's plenty of swap free) when memory usage
> is high.

I've always mistrusted the 2.0.X series x86 SMP kernels in this
regard; however, after I upgraded to 2.0.34 or so I stopped getting
lots of "can't get a free page" errors. (And yes, I had gobs of swap
free.) My machines were most likely to crash when a big process dumped core.

However, rumor has it that the 2.1.X series has much different memory
management. And I wrote some test programs to thrash lots of memory
and others to touch lots and then dump core, and I could never get
them to crash the machine.

-- g



